#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""hao: user commands of the hao builder (workflow plan step 9a and on).

  hao patch OVERLAY... --root ROOT [--state DIR] [--installer] [--dry-run] [--quiet | --show | --log json]
        apply pack overlays to ROOT: only files whose sha256 differs are written, each checked before and
        after; replaced files are backed up and new ones recorded, so --revert undoes both. Without
        --installer, packs whose phase is "installer" (and kernel collection members, dyld cache images, the
        cryptex, the booter and EFI files of any pack) are not applied and are listed as needing Recovery.
  hao patch --revert --root ROOT [--state DIR] [--id ID]      undo the newest (or the named) patch
  hao patch --list [--root ROOT | --live] [--state DIR]       applied and reverted patches
  hao patch --probe [--log json]                              read-only: the running Mac's System and Data
        volumes, snapshots, SIP, mounts, and which steps of a live patch would work
  hao patch OVERLAY... --live [--dry-run] --i-have-a-backup [--bless mount|oclp] [--mount-point DIR]
  hao patch --revert --live --i-have-a-backup
        the running Mac's own System volume (sudo): written directly, booted as a new snapshot after a reboot,
        reverted by booting the snapshot that ran before. Not proven yet (step 9a laptop run): it refuses to
        write without --i-have-a-backup; --dry-run and --probe only read.
  hao explain [NAME | PATH]                                   what a declared file comes from and every edit
  hao inspect STORE PATH                                      original and patched files side by side

  hao detect [--out FILE]                                     this Mac's hardware, OCLP patch sets and packs, as a
        profile file for --profile (run it on the target: its Recovery or an installed macOS; read-only)
  hao fetch RELEASE.json [--cache DIR] [--verify] [--host-check] [--assets-from DIR|URL]
        download (or with --verify only check) every file the release pins: its downloads (Apple's and third-party
        files) and its assets (ours), each by size and sha256, into the content-addressed cache
  hao verify [ROOT] [--manifests DIR|FILE]...                 every installed pack file against its manifest
  hao build-image REPO (--model M | --profile F) [--assembly NAME] [--overlay-system ROOT] [--dry-run]
        assemble an image in the builder VM (pipeline/golden-run), committed to the image repository REPO
  hao assets make DIR (--model M) [--store STORE]           (development) the assets folder a fresh clone builds
        from: every input row the release build reads, at its path below store/, plus the builder's tools, as
        APFS clones; the stand-in for hao fetch plus the on-Mac producers until those run from the downloads
  hao assets check DIR (--model M)                            every input present with its pinned hash
        build-image --assets DIR links a fresh clone's store to DIR and checks it first; --export FILE also writes
        the committed image as one raw disk image file; --here builds on this Mac without the builder VM (an Intel
        Mac started from hao: root, SIP and AMFI relaxed) straight into the raw image file REPO.img
  hao build-disk /dev/diskN (--model M | --profile F) [--kit KIT] [--work DIR] [--local DIR] [--dry-run]
        [--confirm diskN]   (sudo; an external SSD) stage the kit (or take KIT) and build macOS 27 onto the disk
  hao install /dev/diskN --kit KIT [--profile F] [--local DIR] [--dry-run] [--confirm ERASE]
        (on the target, from the installer's Recovery) build macOS 27 from the stick's kit onto this Mac's disk
        Both need root, SIP's filesystem protection off and AMFI relaxed, as the installer's Recovery starts; on a
        Mac's everyday macOS they stop before writing. LOCAL (default WORK/local) holds the assembly's copies and
        logs and must not be on the disk being erased.
  hao create-installer [/dev/diskN] [--model M | --profile F] [--dry-run] [--confirm diskN]  (sudo; USB stick)
        [--kit DIR] [--basesystem DMG] [--opencore ESP.tar] [--image FILE [--image-size BYTES]]
        asks which Mac (a proven model by default, this Mac, or a profile file) and which external disk is the stick,
        and for the disk's name typed; then downloads what release.json (beside hao) pins (nothing with --kit),
        prepares the packs and the kit, and writes the stick: OpenCore + the Recovery on HAOBOOT, hao + the kit on
        HAO (run /Volumes/HAO/install in the stick's Recovery). Not built yet: the kit made from the downloads, so
        without --kit it stages one from this checkout's store. --dry-run asks and shows the plan, nothing more.
  hao report [--output FILE]                                  one text file for a GitHub issue: hao's version, this Mac,
        its hao detect profile, the last command's log and the install record, without serial, user or Wi-Fi names

hao alone (or hao help) lists the commands for where it runs; hao help --all adds the developer commands. A command
run where it doesn't belong says where it runs (HAO_ANYWHERE=1 or --dry-run skips that check). Exit codes: 0 done,
1 failed (stopped safely), 2 usage, 3 not enough space or the wrong drive, 4 a file failed its sha256, 5 refused.

Detail levels for every command: --quiet, default, --show or -v (files), --show-edits (edits), --log json.
On a terminal the step being worked on has a spinner (or a bar) and its time, redrawn in place, with colour; in a
pipe, a log, with NO_COLOR or TERM=dumb the same lines come plain, with a "still working" line every 30 s.
HAO_ASCII=1 draws spinners and bars in ASCII. What the backends print (commands, tool output) goes to the log file
named on failure (the USB installer's logs folder when run from the USB installer), not to the screen.

--state defaults to ROOT/private/var/db/hao, and with --live to /private/var/db/hao. Deferred steps come from
--deferred FILE, else from deferred.json beside the overlays (the materialiser's output folder), when there is one.
"""
